  {
    "episode_id": 71378,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71378.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "I thought it was, I was disappointed what was happening. And I was the district attorney of Staten Island for 12 years, the elected DA before I became a congressman. And I made combating domestic violence and violence against women, the cornerstone of my administration as district attorney. I believe we should take every allegation seriously and investigate it. What troubled me about Dr. Wood's allegations were that the Democratic Party leadership there, Dianne Feinstein, the Senate of California, held the letter that the doctor wrote about her assault for weeks and used it at the end of the hearing as almost a got you moment. If they cared about this woman's well-being, which everyone ought to, they should have disclosed that immediately when it became today retention. They should have asked the FBI to look into it back then, but for nearly two months, they sat on it waiting to use it against then judge Kavanaugh, now justice Kavanaugh, instead of using it to help this woman. So that was what I think was the disappointing part. It's alienated a lot of people. A lot of people feel that this woman was victimized now with another time by being used as a political pawn in this whole process to try to disrupt this confirmation hearing. And I hope we get past it as a nation. This is a great country. Our system works. We're a democracy that's been in existence nearly 250 years. So it's my hope that we get past this as a nation and carry on and continue to people that have been empowered to do the people's work that we continue to do that.",
