  {
    "episode_id": 71378,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71378.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "They are and we're not going to deport millions of people. We're just not going to do that. Our immigration system now is broken, and it needs to be repaired. But this city, this nation, was built by immigrants. Many of them are our ancestors, people of Polish heritage, who have built this city and built this nation. We're all immigrants, some of us are first-generation immigrants, some of us are fifth-generation immigrants, but we're all immigrants. But we're also a nation of laws. So we have to change the laws and fix the laws and see how they're broken and how can we repair them? How can we stop having people coming here illegally and make it easier for people who want to contribute to our society, make it easier for them to come? I sat with a bunch of doctors recently who were telling me about the difficulty with immigrant physicians coming to the United States. Some from Poland who come here and train, and now they're skilled. Now some may want to go back to Poland and use their skills back home, but others who want to stay here because of our visa system, we force them out of the country. These are skilled physicians who want to serve the people here. Yet our immigration system is in such a flux that we force these talented women and men to leave. So something needs to be done. We have to examine it. Sometimes you can't fix a large problem with a big piece of legislation. You have to break it down into smaller pieces of legislation and make sure that they work, particularly things that we could all agree on, and then debate the things that are more difficult.",
