  {
    "episode_id": 71378,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71378.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 58,
    "turn_text": "I am. I think that those in government, like ourselves, take an oath to follow the laws. And sanctuary cities do not follow the federal immigration laws. We may have to change them, but you can't ignore them. When I was the district attorney, I couldn't ignore a certain crime and say, well, I don't think we should prosecute burglaries. You have to follow law once you take an oath of office. If you feel you can't follow those laws, then you shouldn't take that oath of office. But certainly, not cooperating with federal authorities, or as we've recently seen, granting a pardon to someone who committed an offense, granting them a pardon, not because they deserve it, but just so they don't get deported, I don't think what our system is about.",
